<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad ibn Tahir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 862–873</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad ibn Tahir was the last Tahirid governor of Khurasan, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> from 862 to 873 on behalf of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>. Under him the dynasty lost control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> to the Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a> and faced rising disorder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>. In 873 Ya'qub ibn al-Layth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarid dynasty</a> seized Nishapur and captured him, ending Tahirid rule in the east.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad was the son of Tahir ibn Abdallah, governor of Khurasan, and belonged to the Tahirid family that held the province for the Abbasid caliphs. When his father died in 862, the caliph first offered the post to Tahir's brother, Muhammad ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-tahir">Abdallah ibn Tahir</a>, who refused it. The younger Muhammad was appointed instead, but the military governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> (sahib al-shurta), usually held by the Tahirid governor of Khurasan, went to his uncle. He was young and inexperienced, and held office through the civil war of 865–866 under four caliphs, al-Musta'in, al-Mu'tazz, al-Muhtadi and al-Mu'tamid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Only two years after his accession, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> was lost to the Zaydi revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-zayd">Hasan ibn Zayd</a> ibn Muhammad and never recovered. In 867 Ya'qub al-Saffar, the Saffarid amir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and imprisoned its Tahirid governor; an army sent under the Samanid Ibrahim ibn Ilyas was defeated, and Muhammad had to come to terms. He also contested the western offices with his kin, sending another uncle, Sulayman ibn 'Abdallah, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> against 'Ubaydallah, who had taken them over in 867 and later won them back.</p><p>When Ya'qub marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> in 873, Muhammad refused to flee. He spent three years as a prisoner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a>, until caliphal forces defeated them at the Battle of Dair al-'Aqul in 876 and freed him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> restored him as governor of Khurasan, but he never asserted his authority there, although anti-Saffarid leaders such as Ahmad al-Khujistani and Rafi' ibn Harthama placed his name in the khutba in the lands they held.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Muhammad then lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. After Ya'qub's death in 879 his brother Amr bin Laith came to terms with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and was invested with Khurasan in Muhammad's place, named 'Ubaydallah to the western offices and had Muhammad arrested for supposedly backing al-Khujistani, on little evidence. When peace between the caliphate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> broke down around 884, Muhammad regained favour: he was made governor of Baghdad, serving from 885 to 889, and was again named governor of Khurasan without ever ruling it. He died around 910.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad ibn Tahir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Muhammad ibn Tahir</b> the dynasty weakened, and in 873 Ya'qub ibn al-Layth of the Saffarid dynasty seized Nishapur.</blockquote><a href="/state/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.21,58.8&amp;year=867" aria-label="Nishapur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Muhammad ibn Tahir"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4917 4469h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4917 4469h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.21,58.8&amp;year=867">What was here in 867 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
